    },
    {
      "text": "Figure 13: Closer look at Ablation study on the learning rate of REF.",
      "bbox": [
        182.43600463867188,
        393.332763671875,
        429.56793212890625,
        401.3487243652344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We performance Ablation study of varying the learning rate of the REF function to verify the",
      "bbox": [
        107.53199768066406,
        416.4883728027344,
        503.9968566894531,
        425.3949279785156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "importance of the multi-timescale assumption. Particular, we compare our algorithm’s approach of",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        427.3973693847656,
        503.997802734375,
        436.3039245605469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "placing the learning rate of the REF between the policy and lagrange multiplier with making the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        438.3063659667969,
        503.9972229003906,
        447.2129211425781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "REF’s learning rate in various orders of magnitudes slower and faster. Our approach with the learning",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        449.2153625488281,
        504.00140380859375,
        458.1219177246094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "rate satisfying the multi-timescale assumption experimentally appears to still have the best balance",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        460.1243591308594,
        503.99761962890625,
        469.0309143066406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of reward optimization and constraint satisfaction. Particularly when we change the learning rate",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        471.0333557128906,
        503.9974670410156,
        479.9399108886719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "by one order of magnitude (i.e. ×10 or ×0.1), we see the reward performance reduce by around",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        481.22503662109375,
        504.0001525878906,
        490.8489074707031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "half and while the cost violations generally don’t change. But when we change the learning rates by",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        492.8513488769531,
        504.352783203125,
        501.7579040527344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "another order of magnitude, there reward performance effective becomes zero and the cost violations",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        503.7603759765625,
        504.00048828125,
        512.6669311523438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "generally reduce further. By increasing the learning rate of the REF function, we can no longer",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        514.6703491210938,
        504.1701965332031,
        523.576904296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "guarantee that the REF convergences to near the optimally safe REF value. Instead, it becomes the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        525.579345703125,
        503.9986572265625,
        534.4859008789062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "REF of the policy in question. So instead, the optimization can learn to “hack\" the REF function to",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        536.4883422851562,
        504.0010070800781,
        545.3948974609375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "obtain a policy (and lagrange multiplier) that is not a local optimal for the optimization formulation.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        547.3973388671875,
        505.7471008300781,
        556.3038940429688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "On the other hand, when the learning rate is too slow, the lagrange multiplier quickly explodes,",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        558.306396484375,
        505.24725341796875,
        567.2129516601562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "thereby creating a very conservative solution – notice the similarity of the orange line in Figure 13",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        569.21533203125,
        503.994140625,
        578.1218872070312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "with learning rate 0.01 times that of standard in training behavior with PPOLag where χ = 0 in the",
      "bbox": [
        107.64099884033203,
        579.9649658203125,
        504.00360107421875,
        589.0309448242188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ablation study on optimization.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        591.0333251953125,
        231.9844970703125,
        599.9398803710938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
